---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import MigrationSequence from "../components/MigrationSequence.astro";
import ResetSequenceComparison from "../components/ResetSequenceComparison.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";

const ordersSchema = `create table public.orders (
  id          uuid primary key default gen_random_uuid(),
  customer_id uuid not null,
  total       numeric(10, 2) not null,
  status      text not null default 'pending'
);`;

const replaceOrderStatus = `alter table public.orders drop column status;
alter table public.orders add column status_id bigint not null default 1;`;
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Header />
  <main>
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden px-5 pb-16 pt-28 sm:pt-32">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-center">
        <div
          class="text-small mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-brand/25 bg-brand/10 px-3 py-1 text-brand-strong"
        >
          <span class="size-1.5 rounded-full bg-brand"></span>
          Built for reliable Supabase workflows
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-display mx-auto text-balance">
          More control over Supabase resets and dumps.
        </h1>
        <p
          class="text-body mx-auto mt-5 max-w-2xl text-balance text-muted-foreground"
        >
          Supabee turns linked database dumps into reviewable files and replays
          migrations around your seed data in the right order.
        </p>
        <div
          class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-3 sm:flex-row"
        >
          <a
            href="/docs/getting-started/"
            class="text-label inline-flex h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-foreground px-5 text-background shadow-lg transition hover:-translate-y-0.5"
            >Get started</a
          >
          <a
            href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/supabee"
            class="text-label inline-flex h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-xl border border-border bg-card px-5 transition hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-brand/50"
            >View on npm</a
          >
        </div>
        <ResetSequenceComparison class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-2xl text-left" showTitles={false} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="px-5 py-24">
      <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
        <header class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-2xl">
          <p class="text-overline mb-3 uppercase text-brand-strong">
            Why Supabee
          </p>
          <h2 class="text-title">The problems Supabee solves.</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            Supabee is a CLI wrapper around the Supabase CLI. It takes <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabase db reset</code
            > and <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabase db dump</code
            > and makes them cover more cases.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            The four primary problems this solves:
          </p>
          <ol
            class="text-body my-4 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-6 text-muted-foreground"
          >
            <li class="pl-1">
              <code
                class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
                >db reset</code
              > runs all migrations before the seeding scripts, causing errors when
              the data is loaded.
            </li>
            <li class="pl-1">
              <code
                class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
                >db reset</code
              > does not behave like an actual deploy, so problems only show up during
              <code
                class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
                >db push</code
              >.
            </li>
            <li class="pl-1">
              <code
                class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
                >db dump --data-only</code
              > produces one enormous seed file that becomes difficult to review and
              edit.
            </li>
            <li class="pl-1">
              <code
                class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
                >db dump</code
              > produces one enormous schema file, with no per-object files for declarative
              schemas.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h2
            class="text-title mb-4 mt-12 scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-12"
          >
            <a
              href="/docs/database-lifecycle/#reset-a-local-database"
              class="text-foreground no-underline transition hover:text-brand-strong"
              >supabee db reset</a>
          </h2>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            Say you are working on an order inventory system with a table like
            this:
          </p>
          <Code
            code={ordersSchema}
            lang="sql"
            theme="github-dark"
            class="text-code-ui my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 p-5 font-mono shadow-lg"
          />

          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            A new requirement replaces the <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >status</code
            > column, so you write a migration:
          </p>
          <Code
            code={replaceOrderStatus}
            lang="sql"
            theme="github-dark"
            class="text-code-ui my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 p-5 font-mono shadow-lg"
          />

          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            Now <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabase db reset</code
            > runs every migration first, then loads the seed. The seed still fills
            <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >status</code
            >, but the migration has already dropped it:
          </p>
          <pre
            class="text-code-ui my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-code p-5 font-mono text-code-foreground shadow-lg"><code>Applying migration 20260310090000_replace_order_status.sql...
Seeding data from supabase/seed.sql...
ERROR: column "status" of relation "orders" does not exist (SQLSTATE 42703)</code></pre>
          <MigrationSequence variant="reset-conflict" class="my-8" />

          <h3 class="text-heading mb-3 mt-8 scroll-mt-24 text-foreground">
            Inconsistent with <code class="text-code-ui font-mono"
              >db push</code>
          </h3>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            This does not happen in the same order when you deploy. <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabase db push</code
            > and <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabase migration up</code
            > apply the new migration to a database that already contains its data.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >db push</code
            > runs against rows that already exist, while <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >db reset</code
            > expects the seed to fit a schema that changed after the data was dumped.
          </p>
          <MigrationSequence variant="db-push" class="my-8" />

          <h3 class="text-heading mb-3 mt-8 scroll-mt-24 text-foreground">
            The solution
          </h3>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabee db reset</code
            > holds back migrations that have not been deployed and loads the seed
            first. Once the database looks like production, it restores and applies
            the new migrations on top.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            That mirrors how migration scripts reach the production database, so
            one run checks both that the local reset works and that the new
            migrations can be deployed.
          </p>
          <MigrationSequence variant="supabee-reset" class="my-8" />

          <h2
            class="text-title mb-4 mt-12 scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-12"
          >
            <a
              href="/docs/syncing/#sync-data"
              class="text-foreground no-underline transition hover:text-brand-strong"
              >supabee sync data</a>
          </h2>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabase db dump --data-only</code
            > puts every table into one seed file. Once a project contains thousands
            of rows, any table change becomes a diff inside the same enormous file,
            and reproducing a bug starts with finding the right rows inside it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabee sync data</code
            > splits the dump into one file per table, numbered in load order. Large
            tables can span multiple files. Supabee then reconstructs and validates
            the result so you know the split files still load.
          </p>

          <h2
            class="text-title mb-4 mt-12 scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-12"
          >
            <a
              href="/docs/syncing/#sync-schema"
              class="text-foreground no-underline transition hover:text-brand-strong"
              >supabee sync schema</a>
          </h2>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            A schema dump has the same problem: every table, function, type,
            policy, trigger, view, and index arrives in one file.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >supabee sync schema</code
            > keeps the valid ordering from the original dump while splitting it into
            a folder tree with one file per object. An <code
              class="text-code-ui rounded-md border border-border bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-foreground"
              >order-index.json</code
            > records the full sequence so Supabee can rebuild the dump and verify
            that the result still matches.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body my-4 text-muted-foreground">
            You get per-object schema files without having to work out the
            dependency order yourself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <div
        class="mx-auto mt-10 flex max-w-xl items-center gap-3 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card/90 p-2 pl-5 text-left shadow-lg backdrop-blur"
      >
        <span class="select-none font-mono text-brand">$</span>
        <code
          class="text-code-ui min-w-0 flex-1 overflow-x-auto whitespace-nowrap font-mono"
          >npm install -g supabee</code
        >
        <button
          data-copy="npm install -g supabee"
          class="text-small shrink-0 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted px-3 py-2 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground"
          >Copy</button
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-y border-border bg-muted/55 px-5 py-24">
      <div
        class="mx-auto grid max-w-3xl items-center gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[.85fr_1.15fr]"
      >
        <div>
          <p class="text-overline mb-3 uppercase text-brand-strong">
            Predictable by design
          </p>
          <h2 class="text-title">
            The commands you need, without hiding the SQL.
          </h2>
          <p class="text-body mt-5 max-w-xl text-muted-foreground">
            Supabee orchestrates the official Supabase CLI and forwards commands
            it does not own. Your migrations and seed files remain ordinary SQL.
          </p>
          <a
            href="/docs/"
            class="text-label mt-7 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-brand-strong"
            >Explore the documentation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
          >
        </div>
        <div
          class="overflow-hidden rounded-3xl border border-border bg-code text-code-foreground shadow-2xl"
        >
          <div
            class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-white/10 px-5 py-3"
          >
            <span class="size-2.5 rounded-full bg-danger"></span><span
              class="size-2.5 rounded-full bg-warning"></span><span
              class="size-2.5 rounded-full bg-success"></span>
            <span class="text-code-ui ml-2 font-mono text-code-foreground/45"
              >supabee</span
            >
          </div>
          <pre
            class="text-code-ui overflow-x-auto p-6 font-mono text-code-foreground/80"><code><span class="text-brand">$</span> supabee sync data
<span class="text-code-foreground/40">→</span> Checking linked migration alignment
<span class="text-code-foreground/40">→</span> Dumping data from linked project
<span class="text-code-foreground/40">→</span> Splitting data into per-table seed files
<span class="text-code-foreground/40">→</span> Reconstructing and validating output
<span class="text-success">✓</span> Data sync complete</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer class="text-small px-5 py-10 text-muted-foreground">
    <p class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      jacobchak © {new Date().getFullYear()}. All rights reserved.
    </p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
